<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sindh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sindh is a province of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> on the lower course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. It was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, whose city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> lies there. Arab forces under Muhammad ibn al-Qasim conquered it in 711 and 712, and later it was ruled by local dynasties, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the Talpurs and, from 1843, the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sindh.jpg" alt="Sindh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.irfan44 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinnah_Mausoleum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sindh lies in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to the north; to the east it shares a border with the Indian states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Its landscape is mostly alluvial plain on either side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirthar-mountains">Kirthar Mountains</a> in the west. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> is a Persian derivative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> sindhu, meaning river, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> called the Indus Indos. The spelling Sindh replaced the older Sind in 1988.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settled life began around Sindh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> some 9,000 years ago and developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>. Mohenjo-daro, built around 2500 BCE, was one of the largest settlements of that civilisation, with standardised bricks, street grids and covered sewerage. It was abandoned in the 19th century BCE as the civilisation declined, and was not rediscovered until the 1920s. Around 1000 BCE the Indo-Aryan kingdom of Sindhu-Sauvira emerged and lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> captured the region in 518 BCE, after whom came the Macedonians, Mauryans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>.</p><p>The first Muslim naval raids came in 636 under the caliph Umar. Arab forces conquered Sindh in 712 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and made it a province, which laid the foundations of the Muslim period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. The Habbari Emirate emerged in the 9th century, and between the 11th and 16th centuries the Soomra, Samma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a> dynasties ruled, mostly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Akbar, himself born at Umerkot in Sindh, sent an army in 1591 to 1593 to take lower Sindh from the Tarkhan dynasty. Mughal rule ran from the provincial capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a> held upper Sindh from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khudabad">Khudabad</a>. The Talpur dynasty ruled from 1783, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> later captured the province.</p><p>Sindh became a separate province in 1936. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> of 1940 it was the first province to endorse the Pakistan Movement, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of Pakistan in 1947. It was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> in 1955 and restored as a province in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, on the southern coast, is the provincial capital and the largest city. Sindhi, spoken by about 60 per cent of the population at the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, is the most widely spoken language, and about 90 per cent of residents are Muslim. Sufism is an important marker of Sindhi identity for Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> alike, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-abdul-latif-bhittai">Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai</a> lived here. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> lie in the province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> and the Makli Necropolis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sindh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In about 739 the Chalukya governor in Gujarat, Avanijanashraya Pulakeshin, repulsed an Arab raid from <b>Sindh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1749 the Mughal Emperor Ahmad Shah Bahadur ceded <b>Sindh</b>, the Punjab and the trans-Indus lands to save his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 720s and 730s Arab governors of <b>Sindh</b>, notably Junayd, raided deep into western India, and the Gwalior inscription praises Nagabhata I for destroying the armies of a powerful foreign king, a claim usually linked to the repulse of these attacks around 738.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The name Pakistan was coined in January 1933 by the activist Choudhry Rahmat Ali in a pamphlet, Now or Never, as an acronym of Panjab, Afghania, Kashmir, <b>Sindh</b> and Baluchistan; in Persian and Urdu it also means 'the land of the pure'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the 19th century he proclaimed himself the Centennial Reformer of Islam (Mujaddid) and gained a considerable following, especially in the United Provinces, the Punjab and <b>Sindh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ahmadiyya">Ahmadiyya · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Early Harappan phase, from about 3300 BCE, regional traditions named after sites such as Amri in <b>Sindh</b>, Kot Diji and Harappa itself built towns with walls of mud brick and made pottery on the wheel.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
